WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:07.370
TRAVIS WEBB: Welcome to
Mainframe Modernization,

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.800
Accelerating Legacy
Transformation

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.070
With Google Cloud.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.070
Hi, I'm Travis Webb, enterprise
solutions architect at Google.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.210
Thanks for joining me at
Google Cloud, next on air.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.390
I work with our customers to
help them solve their biggest,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.120
most complex IT problems.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.140
And today, I'm going to discuss
mainframe modernization,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.870
a new solution
offering from Google.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.540
This is an introductory talk.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:41.380
So we're going to start out with
an overview of the mainframe

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.950
ecosystem.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.040
I'll then discuss
mainframes' place

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.770
in our world today
and some common ways

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:52.120
that our customers have
tried, often with bad results,

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.520
to migrate off the mainframe.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.340
Next, I'll introduce
Google's offering

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.410
for mainframe modernization.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.950
And finally, we'll walk
through one customer's journey

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.060
as they migrated thousands
of AS400 and mainframe COBOL

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.540
programs to run natively
in Google Cloud.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.450
Let's talk about the
mainframe ecosystem.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:20.870
First, let me give you a quick
overview of the mainframe

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:22.490
market today.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.500
The mainframe market is large.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.540
It includes the costs of
the machines themselves,

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.090
but also the fees paid for
the usage of those machines

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.170
and licenses for mainframe
specific software.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.360
The vast majority
of large companies

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.800
use mainframes to run
critical business workloads.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.380
And many of those
applications have been built

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.150
over the course of decades.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:52.190
Mainframe usage extends beyond
the very largest companies.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.370
And there are many thousands of
mainframe systems still in use.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.740
And each of these
systems represents

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.470
an opportunity for
Google and the customer

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:05.220
to come together to partner
on a strategic transformation

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.680
of their business.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.350
As commodity workloads
are moving to the Cloud

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:15.270
at a rapid pace, mainframe
workloads have lagged behind.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.610
Let's talk about some
of the reasons for this.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.510
It's important to
remember that, for most

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:26.510
of the history of computing,
the idea of running

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.350
important business processes
on a personal computer

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.880
would have you laughed
out of the room.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.610
The PC was a toy.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.400
It was only in this
current Millennium

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.230
that doing important things
on individual PCs or servers

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.280
was taken seriously.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.290
So how is the
mainframe so resistant

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:53.010
to effective competition?

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.700
First, the IBM mainframe
has an unbroken chain

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.320
of backward compatibility
going back to the 1960s.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.780
This durability has
allowed the mainframe

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.620
to amass a large installed
base of customers

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.730
and a reliable revenue
stream for mainframe vendors.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.340
And while the physical
systems from the 1960s

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.060
are no longer in use today,
many of the early programs,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.630
written decades ago,
survive to this day.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.500
Second, our IT leaders
have a tendency

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.845
to closely scrutinize the
entrance costs when evaluating

00:03:28.845 --> 00:03:31.350
a technology
purchasing decision,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.400
while much less attention
is paid to the exit costs.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.480
Leaving the mainframe platform
was expensive and difficult.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.140
And for many businesses,
the cost of migrating still

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.900
isn't economically viable.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.620
A proprietary ecosystem
adds to the high exit costs

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.570
because many
mainframe applications

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.900
rely on proprietary
technologies that

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.890
are not available outside
the mainframe ecosystem.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.530
This combination of factors
leaves current mainframe

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.740
customers with few
viable alternatives.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.650
If the mainframe vendor
has a large installed base

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.530
of customers who can't
leave, why should they

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:16.110
invest in innovation?

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.160
While much improvement occurred
throughout the 20th century,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.060
the relative advantages of the
mainframe over other platforms

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.190
have eroded over time.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.740
And today, mainframe
customers now

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.650
find themselves at a
technological and cost

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:34.710
disadvantage compared
to their peers.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.980
You no longer need a mainframe
to run intensive processing

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.710
tasks, but if you
already have one,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.170
you're stuck paying
for it anyway.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.990
The interesting thing here
is that these disadvantages

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.740
are widely recognized
and understood.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.750
But mainframe customers
had few viable options

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.510
available to assist in
migrating their mainframe

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:57.310
workloads to the Cloud.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.590
With no new customers
or developers

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.590
entering the ecosystem,
mainframe vendors

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:07.750
de-emphasized investment
in innovation.

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:11.680
Of course, around Y2K
companies started figuring out

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.730
how to use commodity servers to
run business workloads, instead

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.410
of mainframes.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:21.100
Google, of course, was at the
forefront of this movement.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.580
It has pioneered many key
data center infrastructure

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.290
innovations.

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:29.530
We serve customers all over
the world with technology

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:31.990
that we've built
into Google Cloud

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.800
and at a scale that simply
would never have been possible

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:36.730
with mainframes.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.840
Meanwhile, public Cloud is
democratizing IT innovations

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.980
at an incredible pace.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:48.670
And its explosive growth
comes from companies

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:51.520
looking to leverage
modern technologies

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.310
that their legacy
vendors aren't providing.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:01.780
It's no secret to anyone
that legacy IT is expensive

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.540
and mainframe often
tops the list.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:06.670
But lots of things
are expensive.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.640
And high operating costs
might be tolerable,

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:13.780
if you also got innovation
and agility in return.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:17.530
But with mainframe, you really
end up with the opposite--

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:21.610
a static ecosystem that
gets more and more expensive

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.370
and makes it harder
and harder to innovate.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:27.740
Customer expectations
are increasing.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:30.080
The pace of innovation
is increasing.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.680
Yet many mainframe customers
see a chronic shortage

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.310
of qualified staff who
understands these technologies,

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:40.520
putting at risk decades of
investment in the platform.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.700
Again, these issues
are widely recognized,

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:50.600
widely understood, most
acutely by mainframe customers

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:51.830
themselves.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:55.210
And many are
saying, we want out.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.580
Companies have tried
many different approaches

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:00.880
over the years to migrate
off the mainframe platform

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:03.970
and often, never quite
achieve escape velocity.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:07.800
And they end up
getting pulled back in.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.890
There are three
main ways customers

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:13.950
attempt to modernize these
legacy systems on their own.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.370
You can undertake a
large manual rewrite

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.310
of all your mainframe software
into the cool new language

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:20.730
of the week.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.690
You can use an emulator to
run your entire mainframe

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.270
environment on normal computers.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:30.050
Or you can modernize in
place, keeping the mainframe,

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.510
but updating the software.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:34.190
We're going over
these approaches

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.920
because they are
all anti-patterns.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.990
And we want to help
customers avoid

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:40.640
these pitfalls in the future.

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:47.040
I've talked to customers in the
middle of a manual rewrite, who

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:50.670
have been modernizing since Y2K.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:54.970
It's expensive, risky,
and it takes forever.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:57.130
And if you work
with developers, you

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:00.250
know it's difficult for them
to resist the temptation

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:04.130
to improve things along the way.

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.310
This, of course, sets
off a chain reaction

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:09.640
of yet more software and
business process changes,

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:11.860
further prolonging the effort.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:16.010
Meanwhile, the technology around
you is changing all the time.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:20.200
The idea of having a clean slate
and a fresh start sounds great.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:22.720
But unfortunately,
experience shows

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.010
that it almost never works.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:31.720
What about emulating the
mainframe environment?

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:34.900
While you can't achieve
true Cloud scale performance

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.360
with a single mainframe,
pound for pound,

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:39.820
it's actually really
good hardware.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:43.150
Most of the problems lie
in the ecosystem layer

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.650
and in the cost structure.

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:48.010
So by emulating your
mainframe software

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:50.260
to run on different
hardware, you're

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:53.200
really throwing away
probably the only good thing

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.080
about the system and
keeping all the stuff that's

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:58.510
actually holding you back.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.520
You incurred many
of the same risks

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.500
as in other
migration approaches,

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.540
but realize few of the benefits.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.190
What about In situ
modernization.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:14.030
We also see companies try
to modernize in place.

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:16.880
They conclude that their own
software is the primary problem

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:18.740
and needs to be updated.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:21.020
At the same time, they
attempted to bring

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.920
pseudo-Cloud capabilities
to the mainframe.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.340
This doesn't solve any of the
problems that cost or talent

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:29.540
scarcity.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:32.270
And it still involves
a large manual effort

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:34.910
to update existing software.

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:41.340
It's a very expensive way
to prolong the inevitable.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.710
At Google, we have a
different perspective.

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:47.270
We talk to our customers.

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:48.860
We feel their pain.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:51.040
And they've been looking
to their Cloud partners

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:52.900
for a way to migrate
these mainframe

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:56.070
workloads to the Cloud.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:59.660
We've built a
solution called G4.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:02.450
It combines Google's
considerable engineering

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:05.660
capabilities with decades
of mainframe modernization

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:07.490
experience.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:11.210
We've developed this expertise
and technology internally,

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.180
as well as through
key acquisitions.

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:16.160
What is G4?

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.480
The G4 platform is a set of
tools developed and proven

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:24.410
over the last 25 years
that transforms your legacy

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:28.670
mainframe applications to
run natively on Google Cloud,

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:34.100
by converting legacy code to
m based on open standards.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:38.360
With G4, we've successfully
taken customers from COBOL

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:42.630
to containers in
a matter of weeks.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:45.870
What was previously too
expensive, too risky,

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:50.420
too difficult, is now
possible at Google scale.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:53.880
And we believe that this is a
unique offering not available

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:54.930
elsewhere in the market.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:01.020
Let's talk about G4 for a
minute and go over what it does

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.110
and how it works.

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:06.600
G4 consists of three
major components--

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.830
the analyzer, the converter,
and the dashboard.

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:14.220
The G4 analyzer parses
your mainframe source code

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:16.650
to understand your
business logic and program

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.230
dependencies in relationships.

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:22.140
The G4 converter
applies conversion rules

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.050
to your mainframe
code and transforms it

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:28.670
into modern Cloud-native Java.

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:31.820
The converter supports dozens
of mainframe languages,

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:38.240
such as COBOL, PL/1 RPG, GCL,
Assembly, and many others,

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:42.680
and can also help modernize
database business logic, IMS

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:44.920
and VSAM.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:48.550
The G4 dashboard provides
performance and complexity

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:52.480
metrics about your software
that assists in the testing

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:56.290
and tuning of the applications.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:58.570
At a high level, our
migration process

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:00.760
follows the tried
and true methodology

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:04.180
that we use for migrating other
mission critical workloads

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:05.920
to Google Cloud.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.200
The insights from
the G4 analyzer

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:11.020
drive the discovery
and assessment phases,

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:14.110
which feed into the G4
converter, which modernizes

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:16.980
legacy mainframe code.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.550
Proper validation,
verification, and testing

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:23.070
ensure that the
business logic operates

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:26.760
as expected and reduces risk.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:30.630
The G4 analyzer parses and
decomposes your sources

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:35.490
into clusters, which informs
the optimal migration strategy.

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:40.260
G4 then converts the mainframe
sources, cluster by cluster,

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:43.530
and produces target
code in Java that is

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:47.020
optimized to run in the Cloud.

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:49.950
This cluster based strategy
allows the customer

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:52.490
to migrate their
applications in waves,

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:54.780
in parallel as the
migration effort,

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.810
using a migration
factory approach.

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:02.370
And I should note here, the code
produced by the G4 converter

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:03.840
is free and clear--

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.810
no license, no lock
in, and no nonsense.

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:12.150
Let me summarize some of
the important benefits

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:14.250
that customers can realize.

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:19.080
With G4, you can reduce costs
and risks, while increasing

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.360
agility and innovation.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:25.500
Costs decrease when you escape
the capital intensive mainframe

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.740
refresh cycle,
eliminate MIPS charges,

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:32.370
and end reliance on
undifferentiated maintenance

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:36.300
toil, just to keep
the mainframe running.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:40.230
Business risk is reduced
by ending reliance

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.500
on a closed ecosystem
of unsupported software

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:46.490
and a chronic talent shortage.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:50.280
G4 increases operational
efficiency, agility,

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:55.380
and innovation by extending the
life of mature software assets,

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:59.340
running them on the Cloud, and
leveraging existing investments

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:01.260
in your software.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.320
By gaining access to
modern Cloud technologies,

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:07.980
you can further optimize,
adapt, and evolve your software

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:10.455
in ways that were not
previously feasible.

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.840
With some of these
benefits in mind,

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:18.190
I'd like to share with you the
journey of one of our mainframe

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:21.850
modernization customers.

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:25.330
We're working with a large
credit data aggregation

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:26.050
company.

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:28.840
You can think of them
as a credit bureau, who

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:32.590
is anticipating a 5x
increase in customer queries

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:35.560
due to recent changes
in legislation.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:37.690
Most of their mission
critical applications

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:39.820
are written in COBOL
and running on both

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:43.360
a mainframe and
AS400 style systems.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:45.850
They're also making use of
other mainframe technologies,

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:51.100
like KIX, JCL,
Easytrieve, and DV2.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.710
Due to the forthcoming
increase in demand,

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:55.390
the customer is
looking to migrate

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:58.990
to a modern, scalable
platform and to modernize

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:03.510
their applications to
support future enhancements.

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:08.340
They had thousands of AS400
and mainframe programs and 12

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:12.210
months to make it happen.

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:15.170
What kinds of workloads
did we migrate?

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:18.120
In mainframe land, there
are two major categories

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:21.310
of workloads, online and batch.

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:24.690
And they have different
architectural requirements.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:28.980
Online programs process requests
and responses in real time

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:33.250
and record those transactions
in a database, as they happen.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:37.710
So if I want to transfer
$100 into my bank account,

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:40.010
I want that to happen now.

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:42.840
And that would be an
online transaction.

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:48.060
This is also known as OLTP, or
online transaction processing.

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:50.580
Batch programs
process book data,

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:53.340
often to aggregate records
generated throughout the day

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:55.320
and perform analyses.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.780
As an example a
batch process might

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:01.590
aggregate all trades that
occur in a given trading day

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.390
and determine a
bank's risk exposure.

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:06.580
Batch programs are
scheduled using

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:08.620
JCL, job control language.

00:16:11.350 --> 00:16:14.620
How did we modernize
JCL batch programs?

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:18.010
Due to the
extensibility of G4, we

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:21.280
can create custom
conversion rules.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:24.410
In this case, we modernized
the customer's JCL

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:28.750
to run a Cloud Composer, a
managed workflow orchestration

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:32.920
service in Google Cloud
based on Apache Airflow.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:35.230
These workflows
trigger batch processes

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:37.790
running on Compute Engine
container optimized

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:42.040
VMs inside a managed
instance group.

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:45.730
These batch processes are Java
executables running the Open

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:49.240
JDK runtime environment.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:53.260
For online workloads, we
deploy the converted mainframe

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:57.070
applications to Compute Engine
inside a managed instance

00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:58.570
group.

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:00.640
Because these
workloads are intended

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:03.220
to be accessed by
partners and customers

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:06.640
over the public internet,
we deployed a load balancer

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:09.280
to direct traffic to the VMs.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:14.349
And we migrated the existing
DB2 database to DB2 LUW

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:16.880
in Compute Engine.

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:19.190
Depending on your
access requirements,

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:23.210
you might instead deploy a VPN
or an interconnect to your data

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:27.470
center for secure communication
within your corporate network.

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:29.990
And depending on your
transaction processing

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:33.770
requirements, we could
deploy DB2 LUW directly

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:36.800
to Google Cloud or
we can re-platform

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:39.200
your relational
database to PostgreSQL

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:41.610
and deploy our Cloud
SQL managed service.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:48.150
OK, we've covered a pretty wide
range of topics in this talk,

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:51.420
from computing history
to Cloud economics

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.420
to mainframe deployment
architectures.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:57.150
So here are a few
key points I'd like

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:59.970
to highlight before you go.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:04.990
G4 is a true, end to end,
mainframe migration solution

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:06.640
from Google.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:10.240
It enables our customers
to reduce costs and risks,

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:13.560
while increasing
agility and innovation.

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:16.540
Proofs of concept are in
high demand by our customers,

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:21.550
due to the potential this has
to transform their business.

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:25.030
And importantly, Google
is the only Cloud provider

00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:28.085
with an integrated, end to
end mainframe modernization

00:18:28.085 --> 00:18:28.585
solution.

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:35.430
Thank you for joining me today
at Google Cloud, Next On Air.

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.290
I look forward to
working with you

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:40.880
on your mainframe
modernization journey.

